.TH PANEL 1 "October 2026" "ServerPanel 1.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
panel \- terminal administration panel for Debian 13
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sudo panel
.br
.B panel motd
.br
.B panel --version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B panel
is a keyboard-first terminal interface for systemd services, Apache
VirtualHosts, cron jobs and host metrics. Interactive administration
requires root. No web interface or extra network port is required.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B LEFT / RIGHT
Switch section; move between the actions of the selected item.
.TP
.B UP / DOWN
Select a service, site, cron job or action.
.TP
.B ENTER
Focus the action pane, then run the selected action.
.TP
.B TAB
Move focus between the list and the actions.
.TP
.B 1 / 2 / 3 / 4
Jump to VPS, SERVICES, SITES or CRON. The panel opens on VPS.
.TP
.B ESC
Return to the list or close a dialog.
.TP
.B N
Create a service, site or cron job, depending on the section.
.TP
.B F
Pin or unpin the selected service or cron job.
.TP
.B A
Show all systemd units or only the followed ones, or, in the CRON section,
every cron instead of the managed jobs and favourites.
.TP
.B R
Refresh inventories and metrics.
.TP
.B Q
Quit.
.SH CRON
The CRON section lists every job found in
.IR /etc/crontab ,
.IR /etc/cron.d/* " and"
.IR /var/spool/cron/crontabs/* ,
plus the jobs managed by the panel. The default view shows the managed jobs
and the favourites only: system crons that are not pinned are hidden.
Press
.B F
to pin or unpin the selected job; favourites are marked with a star and set
apart above a separator. Press
.B A
to switch to every cron, and again to return to the managed jobs and
favourites. Managed jobs are stored in
.I /etc/serverpanel/config.json
under the
.B crons
key. Favourites are carried by the cron entries themselves: a managed entry is
pinned with a
.B pinned
flag, while a pinned external cron is materialised under an
.I ext:<hash>
key with
.B ext
and
.B pinned
set to true. The order of the
.B crons
keys is the favourite display order. The former
.B pinned_crons
key is migrated automatically; pinned external entries are never projected to
.IR /etc/cron.d/serverpanel .
Managed jobs are written to
.IR /etc/cron.d/serverpanel ,
which is regenerated on every change; manual edits of that file are
overwritten. The assistant builds a schedule from daily, weekly, monthly,
every N hours or every N minutes choices, or from a custom five-field cron
expression. A managed job can be edited, run immediately, enabled/disabled,
inspected through the CRON journal, or deleted. External crontabs are
read-only: only information and journal actions are offered. They are hidden
by default; press
.B A
to show them, then
.B F
to pin one and keep it in the favourite view. They are labelled from what
identifies them: the file name for
.IR /etc/cron.d/* ,
the run-parts period for
.I /etc/crontab
lines (cron.daily, cron.weekly, ...), and the account for user crontabs.
.SH FILES
.I /etc/serverpanel/config.json
.br
.I /etc/update-motd.d/85-serverpanel
.br
.I /etc/systemd/system/panel-*.service
.br
.I /etc/apache2/sites-available/panel-*.conf
.br
.I /etc/cron.d/serverpanel
.SH SECURITY
Application processes run under dedicated unprivileged system users.
No administrative command is executed via a shell, except the immediate
run of a cron job, which uses
.B /bin/sh -c
the same way cron does. Cron files written by the panel are root-only, and
commands and schedules are validated. Critical networking/SSH
units are protected from stop/disable operations. Use the system console for
emergency maintenance. Always back up the server before large changes.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR apache2ctl (8),
.BR certbot (7),
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR cron (8),
.BR crontab (5)
